---
// REVIEW: Layer 2 interpretation for the Vanderbilt Orthostatic Symptom Score
// (VOSS), drafted for Craig's review. Static, rendered at build time. FAQ
// answers come from vanderbilt-orthostatic-symptom-score.content.ts and are
// shown word for word (they also feed the page's FAQPage JSON-LD).
import { FAQ, REFERENCE, SOURCES } from '../../tools/vanderbilt-orthostatic-symptom-score.content';
import '../../styles/interpretation.css';

const s = SOURCES;
---

<!-- REVIEW: new copy for Craig's review. -->
<section class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="understanding-voss">
	<h2 id="understanding-voss">Understanding your VOSS score</h2>

	<h3 id="what-it-measures">What the score measures</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.measures.a}</p>
	<p>
		It first appeared in Vanderbilt drug trials (<a href={s.raj2005.href} rel="noopener">{s.raj2005.label}</a>;
		<a href={s.raj2009.href} rel="noopener">{s.raj2009.label}</a>). Because it is a snapshot, this page records when you
		rated it and only compares results rated the same way.
	</p>
	<p>{FAQ.compare.a}</p>

	<h3 id="score-bands">Score bands</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.high.a}</p>
	<table class="interp-table">
		<caption>VOSS totals reported in published studies</caption>
		<thead>
			<tr><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">When rated</th><th scope="col">People</th><th scope="col">Total</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{REFERENCE.map(r => (
				<tr><th scope="row">{r.group}</th><td>{r.when}</td><td>{r.n}</td><td>{r.value}</td><td><a href={s[r.source].href} rel="noopener">{s[r.source].label}</a></td></tr>
			))}
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h3 id="change">What a change in score means</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.change.a}</p>
	<p>
		Keep the conditions the same each time: time of day, posture and time standing. Rating at rest and again at the end of
		a stand shows how much standing adds. The <a href="/tools/trackers/stand-test-logger/">stand test logger</a> can hold
		the matching heart rate readings.
	</p>

	<h3 id="what-it-does-not-measure">What it does not measure</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.diagnosis.a}</p>
	<p>
		The authors of a 2024 breathing study note that the VOSS lacks psychometric validation and that one number can hide
		symptoms that change during the stand (<a href={s.stick.href} rel="noopener">{s.stick.label}</a>). For heart rate,
		use the <a href="/tools/nasa-lean-test/">NASA lean test</a> and the
		<a href="/evidence/30-bpm-threshold-pots/">30 bpm threshold explainer</a>.
	</p>

	<h3 id="taking-it-to-your-gp">Taking this to your GP or consultant</h3>
	<p>
		Bring the total, the date, how you rated it and the heart rate readings from the same stand. A pair of ratings, at rest
		and after 10 minutes upright, says more than one number. Describe the VOSS as a nine-symptom rating used in POTS
		treatment trials. It shows how much standing affects you and whether that changes with treatment, but it is not a
		diagnostic test. See
		<a href="/nhs/preparing-for-gp-appointment/">preparing for a GP appointment</a>.
	</p>

	<div class="tool-foot">
		<slot name="disclaimer" />
		<details class="sources" id="sources">
			<summary>Sources and licence</summary>
			<slot name="citation" />
			<ul class="interp-sources">
				<li>Raj SR, Black BK, Biaggioni I, Harris PA, Robertson D. Acetylcholinesterase inhibition improves tachycardia in postural tachycardia syndrome. <em>Circulation</em> 2005;111(21):2734–2740. <a href={s.raj2005.href} rel="noopener">Article</a></li>
				<li>Raj SR, Black BK, Biaggioni I, et al. Propranolol decreases tachycardia and improves symptoms in the postural tachycardia syndrome: less is more. <em>Circulation</em> 2009;120(9):725–734. <a href={s.raj2009.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
				<li>Stick M, et al. Deep abdominal breathing reduces heart rate and symptoms during orthostatic challenge in patients with postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome. <em>Eur J Neurol</em> 2024;31:e16402. <a href={s.stick.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
				<li>Bourne KM, et al. Abdominal-only compression garments reduce orthostatic tachycardia and improve symptoms in patients with postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome. <em>Can J Cardiol</em> 2026;42(6):1320. <a href={s.bourne.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
				<li>Pereira TJ, Edgell H. N95 masks increase brain blood velocity and parasympathetic outflow, yet worsen orthostatic symptoms in a healthy cohort. <em>Physiol Rep</em> 2025;13:e70402. <a href={s.pereira.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
			</ul>
		</details>
	</div>
</section>
